{"attack":true,"case":"The target's conclusion that audit flags are 'sustained' is unsupported by the evidence it cites, because that evidence shows only structural similarity (two stances) and never establishes the substantive predicate required by §11.3: that both stances converge on falsificationism.\nThe target asserts the flags are sustained because the two school policies 'implicitly converge on falsificationist principles.' Yet the cited support content contains no mention of falsificationism — it records only school labels and stances ('mechanist' and 'skeptic'). The convergence claim is therefore an inferential leap with no grounding in the provided evidence. Under the audit-ritual standard, a sustained adjudication must rest on demonstrated conformance to the ritual's criteria; here, the target substitutes an unsupported assertion about implicit convergence for any actual showing that either policy adopts falsificationist principles. Because the pivotal premise is absent from the support chain, the conclusion that the flags are sustained is non-sequitur and fails the problem as stated.\ncites: e6fb997c9be7399fe792edaac31ebbed54b23bfc511c501e4276d08d2cfcfb0f","premise":"The support chain provides no evidence that either school policy is falsificationist; it only labels them as 'mechanist' and 'skeptic'.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","quote":"{\"school_policy\": {\"school\": \"school-0\", \"stance\": \"mechanist\"}}"},{"block":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","quote":"{\"school_policy\": {\"school\": \"school-1\", \"stance\": \"skeptic\"}}"}]}